---
import BaseLayout from "../components/layout/BaseLayout.astro";
import { loadDataset } from "../lib/data";
import { SUM_TOLERANCE } from "../lib/schema";

const { predictions, meta, isMock } = loadDataset();
const pipe = meta.pipeline;
const fmt = new Intl.NumberFormat("en-GB");
const fieldSizes = [...new Set(predictions.map((p) => p.drivers.length))].sort((a, b) => a - b);

const contents = [
  { id: "model", label: "The model" },
  { id: "data", label: "The data" },
  { id: "snapshots", label: "Two snapshots" },
  { id: "predicted", label: "What is predicted" },
  { id: "scoring", label: "Scoring" },
  { id: "rejected", label: "Tried and rejected" },
  { id: "evaluation", label: "Evaluation" },
  { id: "bugs", label: "Two scorer bugs" },
  { id: "limits", label: "What it cannot see" },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Methodology"
  description="What Sector 4 predicts, which data it is built on, how each prediction is scored against the result, and what the model cannot see."
  image="/og/methodology.png"
  breadcrumbs={[{ name: "Methodology", path: "/methodology/" }]}
>
  <section class="wrap page-hero">
    <p class="eyebrow">Methodology</p>
    <h1>How the numbers are made.</h1>
    <p class="lead">
      A prediction you cannot check is a claim, not a forecast. Everything below is here so the
      numbers on this site can be argued with.
    </p>
    <nav aria-label="On this page" class="contents">
      {contents.map((c, i) => (
        <a class="btn btn-soft rise" style={`--i:${i}`} href={`#${c.id}`}>
          {c.label}
        </a>
      ))}
    </nav>
  </section>

  <section id="model" class="wrap band chapter">
    <h2 class="h3">What the model is, and what it is not.</h2>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        The model is a logistic regression with <strong>four inputs</strong>: where the driver
        qualified, their best practice lap relative to the fastest car, their championship points,
        and their share of their team's points. Qualifying position enters as a smooth curve rather
        than a straight line, because the gap between first and second is not the gap between
        fifteenth and sixteenth.
      </p>
      <p>
        That is the post-qualifying model. Before the cars run, qualifying and practice do not
        exist, so the pre-weekend snapshot reduces to the two inputs that do: championship points
        and share of team points. It is a weaker model, not a different view of the same one.
      </p>
      <p>
        Four inputs is not a simplification made for the sake of the explanation. Larger versions
        were built and measured — nineteen features, twenty-two, thirty-three — and every one of
        them scored worse. Circuit history, tyre data and driver-against-teammate form were all
        tried, and all three made the predictions less accurate rather than more.
      </p>
      <div class="callout">
        <p>
          <strong>The model has not been shown to beat a simple baseline.</strong> The baseline is
          qualifying order: the historical chance of winning from each grid slot, and nothing else.
          Across 2019–2025 the model scored better, but that comparison is what chose the model, so
          it flatters it. The one clean out-of-sample test — the 2026 season, held back from every
          decision and scored once — was <strong>inconclusive</strong>. The model was ahead on
          average, and well inside the range that fourteen races of a regulation-reset season cannot
          separate from chance.
        </p>
      </div>
      <p>
        So this site does not claim that the model beats qualifying order. What it does claim is
        that the win probabilities are well calibrated — when it says 20%, that happens about one
        time in five — and <a href="/track-record/">the track record</a> prints the baseline's score
        beside the model's on every race, so the question is yours to settle rather than ours.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  {isMock && (
    <section class="wrap band chapter">
      <h2 class="h3">What is running right now.</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <div class="callout">
          <p>
            <strong>The numbers on these pages are sample data, not the model's output.</strong> The
            model described above exists and has been measured, but this site is still showing
            probabilities generated to exercise the layout — a Plackett–Luce draw over a plausible
            field, scored against invented results. They say nothing about any real race.
          </p>
        </div>
        <p>
          Real predictions are being written one race at a time, each committed before the session
          it predicts and scored afterwards in a separate step, so the record cannot be edited once
          the result is known. This page will lose this notice when those reach the site. The banner
          at the top of every page and the label on every link preview come from the same flag, so
          neither can be forgotten.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>
  )}

  <section id="data" class="wrap band chapter">
    <h2 class="h3">The data.</h2>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        Results, qualifying, grids, laps, pit stops and status codes come from the Ergast schema:
        the Kaggle Formula 1 World Championship export for 1950 to 2024, and the Jolpica F1 API for
        2025 onward. Session telemetry, timing and weather from 2018 onward come from FastF1.
      </p>
      <p>Five rules govern that pipeline, and they are enforced rather than intended.</p>
      <dl class="defs">
        <div class="rise" style="--i:0">
          <dt>Nothing is invented.</dt>
          <dd>
            A missing value stays missing and is flagged. No interpolation, no back-filling, no
            plausible substitutes.
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div class="rise" style="--i:1">
          <dt>Provenance travels with the row.</dt>
          <dd>
            Every record carries the source it came from, so any figure can be traced back to the
            file it was read from.
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div class="rise" style="--i:2">
          <dt>Conflicts are logged, not resolved quietly.</dt>
          <dd>
            Where two sources disagree, both values are recorded with the evidence, and the
            resolution is a documented decision rather than a silent preference.
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div class="rise" style="--i:3">
          <dt>Time is UTC.</dt>
          <dd>
            Stored in UTC, rendered in your zone by the page. Original local date fields are kept as
            they were.
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div class="rise" style="--i:4">
          <dt>Builds are deterministic.</dt>
          <dd>
            The same inputs produce byte-identical outputs, which is what makes a change reviewable
            at all.
          </dd>
        </div>
      </dl>
      {pipe && (
        <>
          <h3 class="title sub-h">What that adds up to</h3>
          <div class="scroller">
            <table class="table">
              <caption class="sr-only">
                Row counts per table, split by the source each row came from.
              </caption>
              <thead>
                <tr>
                  <th scope="col">Table</th>
                  <th scope="col" class="n">
                    Rows
                  </th>
                  <th scope="col" class="n">
                    Kaggle
                  </th>
                  <th scope="col" class="n">
                    Jolpica
                  </th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                {Object.entries(pipe.tables).map(([name, t], i) => (
                  <tr class="rise" style={`--i:${i}`}>
                    <th scope="row">{name.replace(/_/g, " ")}</th>
                    <td class="n num">{fmt.format(t.rows)}</td>
                    <td class="n num dim">{fmt.format(t.by_source.kaggle ?? 0)}</td>
                    <td class="n num dim">{fmt.format(t.by_source.jolpica ?? 0)}</td>
                  </tr>
                ))}
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
          <dl class="figures">
            <div>
              <dt>Seasons covered</dt>
              <dd class="num">
                {pipe.seasons.first}–{pipe.seasons.last}
              </dd>
            </div>
            <div>
              <dt>Validation checks</dt>
              <dd class="num">
                {pipe.validation.passed ?? "—"} passed, {pipe.validation.failed ?? "—"} failed
              </dd>
            </div>
            <div>
              <dt>Corrections applied</dt>
              <dd class="num">{pipe.corrections.total}</dd>
            </div>
            <div>
              <dt>Unresolved conflicts</dt>
              <dd class="num">{pipe.unresolved_conflicts}</dd>
            </div>
          </dl>
          <p>
            Of those corrections,{" "}
            <span class="num">{pipe.corrections.by_evidence.official ?? 0}</span> cite an official
            source — an FIA classification document or a formula1.com results page — and{" "}
            <span class="num">{pipe.corrections.by_evidence.internal ?? 0}</span> cite an internal
            derivation, where the dataset contradicts itself and the resolution follows from other
            rows rather than from an outside document. Both kinds are recorded with what changed and
            why. Session timing from <span class="num">{pipe.enrichment_first_season}</span> onward
            is layered on top from FastF1, and is used to fit the model but never published — see{" "}
            <a href="/credits/">credits</a>.
          </p>
        </>
      )}
      <p>
        The current data version is <span class="num">{meta.data_version}</span>. Sources and
        licences are listed on <a href="/credits/">the credits page</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="snapshots" class="wrap band chapter">
    <h2 class="h3">Two snapshots per race.</h2>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        A prediction is only meaningful with a timestamp on it, so each race gets up to two. Neither
        is changed after its deadline: the first session of the weekend for pre-weekend, the start
        of the race for post-qualifying. A correction before then is published as a new revision
        beside the original, and the race page shows both.
      </p>
      <dl class="defs">
        <div>
          <dt>Pre-weekend</dt>
          <dd>
            Published before any car runs. It uses two inputs, championship points and share of team
            points, and knows nothing about qualifying or practice. Scored beside championship
            order.
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>Post-qualifying</dt>
          <dd>
            Published after qualifying, with the qualifying order and this weekend's practice pace
            known. It uses qualifying position rather than the grid, so penalties applied afterwards
            are not in it; <a href="#limits">why is below</a>. This is the sharper of the two and
            the one a race page shows by default. Scored beside qualifying order.
          </dd>
        </div>
      </dl>
      <p>
        Both remain on the race page afterwards. Comparing them is the honest way to see how much of
        the prediction was qualifying and how much was everything else.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="predicted" class="wrap band chapter">
    <h2 class="h3">What is predicted.</h2>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>For every driver in the field, each snapshot gives:</p>
      <dl class="defs">
        <div>
          <dt>Win, podium, top ten</dt>
          <dd>
            The probability of finishing there or better. Only the win probability has been
            validated; podium and top ten follow from it through one simulation of the whole race.
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>DNF</dt>
          <dd>
            The probability of not being classified as a finisher. It is fitted separately from the
            rest and has not been validated.
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>Expected position</dt>
          <dd>
            The mean of the driver's full finishing-position distribution, not a rounded guess at a
            place. The distribution itself is drawn on each race page.
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>Top factors</dt>
          <dd>
            The handful of inputs moving that driver's number most, with direction and relative
            weight. Weights only, never a lap time or any timing value.
          </dd>
        </div>
      </dl>
      <p>
        Those numbers have to hold together, so the build checks them rather than trusting them.
        Across a field of {fieldSizes.join(" or ")} drivers, win probabilities must sum to one,
        podium probabilities to three and top-ten probabilities to ten; each driver's position
        distribution must sum to one; and no driver may be likelier to win than to podium, or
        likelier to podium than to finish in the top ten. The tolerance is{" "}
        <span class="num">{SUM_TOLERANCE.toExponential(0)}</span>, which is float noise and nothing
        more. A file that fails any of these stops the build instead of reaching a page.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="scoring" class="wrap band chapter">
    <h2 class="h3">How a prediction is scored.</h2>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        Scoring happens once, against the latest revision published before that snapshot's deadline,
        and the result is never recomputed. A revision published after its deadline is listed on the
        race page and never scored. Four numbers per race:
      </p>
      <dl class="defs">
        <div>
          <dt>Log loss</dt>
          <dd>
            <span class="num">−ln p</span>, where <span class="num">p</span> is the probability
            given to the driver who actually won. It punishes confident misses hard, which is the
            point: a model that says 60% and is wrong should hurt more than one that says 20% and is
            wrong. Lower is better.
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>Brier score</dt>
          <dd>
            The squared error of the whole win distribution, summed over every driver, with one for
            the winner and zero for everybody else. Unlike log loss it is bounded, so a single
            catastrophic race cannot dominate a season. Lower is better.
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>Winner called</dt>
          <dd>
            Whether the driver given the highest win probability won. Crude, and included because it
            is the number everyone actually checks.
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>Podium places</dt>
          <dd>
            How many of the three drivers given the highest podium probability finished on the
            podium, out of three.
          </dd>
        </div>
      </dl>
      <p>
        A log loss means nothing without a reference, so{" "}
        <a href="/track-record/">the track record</a> prints two: what a uniform random pick over
        the same field would have scored, which is the floor, and what a baseline that saw the same
        information scored, which is the bar. That is qualifying order for the post-qualifying
        snapshot and championship order for the pre-weekend one. Both are computed the same way as
        the model's own number, on the same race, from data available before it started.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="rejected" class="wrap band chapter">
    <h2 class="h3">What was tried and rejected.</h2>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        The four features are not a first guess that happened to work. Larger and cleverer models
        were built, measured against the same baseline on the same races, and found worse.
      </p>
      <dl class="defs">
        <div>
          <dt>Nine features that measured as noise</dt>
          <dd>
            Circuit history, tyre data and driver-against-teammate form — nine columns across three
            groups. Not one of them differed from zero at 95% confidence, and adding them moved the
            log loss the wrong way: 1.3286 with form alone, 1.3480 once circuit history was added,
            1.3765 once tyre data was.
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>A gradient-boosted tree model</dt>
          <dd>
            It memorised its training set — 0.0069 log loss on data it had seen against 0.2155 on
            data it had not. Regularising it heavily closed that gap to 0.0256 and it still scored
            worse than the simple model at race level.
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>An Elo rating</dt>
          <dd>
            2.0256 against the baseline's 1.4340. A rating that sees only the driver cannot see the
            car, and in this sport the car is most of the answer.
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>A ranking model with simulated race orders</dt>
          <dd>
            It produced a coherent joint distribution, which the four marginals do not, but it lost
            to the baseline by a margin that did not depend on chance. Its simulation still supplies
            the position distributions shown on race pages.
          </dd>
        </div>
      </dl>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="evaluation" class="wrap band chapter">
    <h2 class="h3">How the model is evaluated.</h2>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        Each race is predicted by a model fitted on races strictly before it and on nothing else.
        That is slow — the model is refitted for every race — and it is the only way the comparison
        against a baseline that works the same way means anything.
      </p>
      <dl class="defs">
        <div>
          <dt>Bootstrap intervals, resampled over races</dt>
          <dd>
            Not over driver rows. Twenty drivers in one race are not twenty pieces of evidence: if
            the model misreads the car, it misreads all twenty at once.
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>Paired against the baseline</dt>
          <dd>Race by race, so a run of unusually hard races cannot decide the comparison.</dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>Calibration beside discrimination</dt>
          <dd>
            Discrimination asks whether the ordering is right. Calibration asks whether a stated 20%
            happens about one time in five. This site publishes the number itself, so the second
            question is the one that matters, and a model with a better log loss and worse
            calibration would be the wrong choice here.
          </dd>
        </div>
      </dl>
      <div class="callout">
        <p>
          The evidence that the model beats the baseline is <strong>in-sample</strong>. The
          comparison across 2019–2025 is what chose this model, so it flatters it. The one clean
          test — 2026, held back from every decision and scored once — was inconclusive.{" "}
          <a href="/track-record/">The track record</a> is the real ongoing test, and it starts
          empty.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="bugs" class="wrap band chapter">
    <h2 class="h3">Two bugs in the scorer, and how they were caught.</h2>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        Both were found by the cheapest thing in the project, and both would have quietly flattered
        every number measured afterwards.
      </p>
      <dl class="defs">
        <div>
          <dt>A uniform guess scored a 99.4% hit rate</dt>
          <dd>
            The field arrives sorted by finishing position, so picking the highest probability fell
            back on row order and handed a tied predictor the winner every time. Breaking ties by
            driver id instead gave 19.9% — still wrong, because the lowest id in this era belongs to
            a driver who won a fifth of those races. Ties are now settled by expectation, and a
            uniform guess scores exactly one in twenty, which is arithmetic rather than judgement.
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>A ranking model gave the actual winner zero</dt>
          <dd>
            It scored five times worse than a random guess, assigning a near-certainty to one driver
            in most races, because its confidence was tuned on the same races it had learned from.
            Every accuracy metric looked healthy while this happened — the winner hit rate sat at
            52%. Only the probabilities showed it.
          </dd>
        </div>
      </dl>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="limits" class="wrap band chapter">
    <h2 class="h3">What this cannot predict.</h2>
    <div class="prose">
      <dl class="defs">
        <div>
          <dt>Safety cars and first-lap incidents</dt>
          <dd>
            A large part of what separates the prediction from the result, and irreducible rather
            than a gap to be closed.
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>Weather on the day</dt>
          <dd>
            A dry forecast that turns wet rearranges a race, and a probability published on a
            Tuesday cannot know that.
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>Reliability</dt>
          <dd>
            Modelled as a rate, which is the right shape for a season and useless for a particular
            car on a particular afternoon.
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>Anything not in the data</dt>
          <dd>Team orders, an undisclosed upgrade, a driver racing unwell.</dd>
        </div>
      </dl>
      <p>
        There is also one feature deliberately missing. <strong>Grid position</strong> is strictly
        better information than qualifying position because it includes penalties, and it is
        knowable before the race starts — but there is no feed that would let the model use it live
        as well as in training. Training on it and serving something else would make every score on
        this site an overstatement, so it stays out until that feed exists.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .contents {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: var(--space-xs);
    margin-top: var(--space-md);
  }

  .sub-h {
    margin-top: var(--space-md);
  }
</style>
